🚨 AWS WAF vs. Shield vs. Firewall Manager — Which One Protects What?
 
All three are key players in AWS security, but they serve different purposes depending on what you're trying to protect and how centralized you want your policies to be.

 Here’s a quick and clear comparison:

🛡️ AWS WAF (Web Application Firewall)
 ✔️ Filters HTTP/S traffic at Layer 7.
 ✔️ Lets you block IPs, bots and SQL.
 ✔️ Customizable rules via rule groups or managed rules
 🧠 Use case: Protecting APIs, ALBs and CloudFront from common web exploits.

⚔️ AWS Shield
 ✔️ Protects against DDoS attacks at Layers 3, 4 and 7.
 ✔️ Shield Standard is automatic and free.
 ✔️ Shield Advanced offers cost protection, reports and tuning.
 🧠 Use case: Mitigating large-scale DDoS attacks on critical apps.

🏢 AWS Firewall Manager
 ✔️ Centralizes security policy enforcement across accounts.
 ✔️ Works with WAF, Shield Advanced and security groups.
 ✔️ Coordinates rules across Layers 3 to 7.
 🧠 Use case: Organizations with multiple accounts needing consistent security policies.

🎯 In short:
 Want to block malicious HTTP/S traffic? ➡️ Use AWS WAF
 Need automatic DDoS protection? ➡️ Start with Shield Standard
 Managing org-wide security policies? ➡️ Go with Firewall Manager
